<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s09e07" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 7: Slope-Intercept Form, Part 7</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 7 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a slope and a point on a line, find the line’s equation.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given two points on a line, find the line’s equation.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    If you know the slope of a line and one point the line passes through, you can find the line’s equation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>How to Find the Equation of a Line:</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          	Set up the slope-intercept form template <m>y=mx+b</m>. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>	
	In the template, the letter <m>m</m> stands for the slope. Find the slope <m>m</m> using one of the techniques or formulas from Episode 5.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Let <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> stand for a point the line passes through. Plug <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> into the template and the number for the slope <m>m</m> into the template
          and solve for <m>b</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          	Plug your values of <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> back into the slope-intercept form template. 
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      A line has a slope of <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> and it passes through the point <m>(3,1)</m>. Find the graph’s equation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Set up the slope-intercept form template:
      <md>
        y=mx+b
      </md>
      In this problem the slope, <m>m=\frac{1}{4}</m> , is given to us directly, so we don’t have to calculate it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The point that the line passes through is <m>(3,1)</m>. Plug the given value of m and the point <m>(x_0,y_0)=(3,1)</m> into the slope-intercept template:
      <md>
        1 = \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)(3)+b
      </md>
      Now let’s solve this equation for <m>b</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>2  \amp = \frac{3}{4} + b </mrow>
        <mrow>2-\frac{3}{4} \amp = b </mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{8}{4} - \frac{3}{4} \amp = b </mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{5}{4} \amp = b </mrow>
      </md>
      Now, plug <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> back into the slope-intercept template:
      <md>
        y = \frac{1}{4}x + \frac{5}{4}
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
  <p>
    A line passes through the two points <m>(2,5)</m> and <m>(4,1).</m> Find the equation of the line.
    </p>
      <p>
      Solution 1: Start by using the two points to find the line’s slope:
      <md>
        m = \frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}
      </md>
      <md>
        m = \frac{1-5}{4-2}
      </md>
      <md>
        m = -\frac{4}{2}
      </md>
      <md>
        m = -2
      </md>
      Now plug this value of m and the point <m>(x,y)=(2,5)</m> into the slope-intercept template to find <m>b</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>y \amp = mx+b </mrow>
        <mrow>5 \amp = -2(2) + b </mrow>
        <mrow>5 \amp = -4 + b </mrow>
        <mrow>5 +4 \amp = b </mrow>
        <mrow>9 \amp =  b </mrow>
      </md>
      Finally, put our values of <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> back into the template:
      <md>
        y = -2x+9
      </md>
      
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 2: Start by using the two points to find the line’s slope:
      <md>
        m = \frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}
      </md>
      <md>
        m = \frac{1-5}{4-2}
      </md>
      <md>
        m = -\frac{4}{2}
      </md>
      <md>
        m = -2
      </md>
      Now plug this value of m and the point <m>(x,y)=(4,1)</m> into the slope-intercept template to find <m>b</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>y \amp = mx+b </mrow>
        <mrow>1 \amp = -2(4) + b </mrow>
        <mrow>1 \amp = -8 + b </mrow>
        <mrow>1+8 \amp = b </mrow>
        <mrow>9 \amp =  b </mrow>
      </md>
      Finally, put our values of <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> back into the template:
      <md>
        y = -2x+9
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s09e07">
    <title>Season 9, Episode 7 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Find the equation of the line given a slope and a points
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            slope = 4, point = <m>(-2,1)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            slope = -5, point = <m>(7,3)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            slope = 1, point = <m>(0,19)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            slope = <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>, point = <m>(3,-8)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            slope = 0.25, point = <m>(3,4.5)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Find an equation of the line given a pair of points.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(1,4), (3,9)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(3,6), (8,0)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(-2,4), (3,-1)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(4,-5), (1,-2)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    
  </worksheet>

</section>  